import{_ as p,E as a,h as i,c as u,b as r,f as o,H as t,a4 as s,w as n,l as h,a3 as m}from"./chunks/framework.B_fYP6Of.js";const x=JSON.parse('{"title":"Robert C. Martin principles review","description":"An independent reading of Clean Architecture applied to run ownership, evidence publication, and the boundary between application rules and infrastructure.","frontmatter":{"title":"Robert C. Martin principles review","description":"An independent reading of Clean Architecture applied to run ownership, evidence publication, and the boundary between application rules and infrastructure.","teaser":"If you replaced the HTTP framework or database, which rule would still stop an obsolete answer from being published?"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"reviews/clean-architecture.md","filePath":"reviews/clean-architecture.md"}'),b={name:"reviews/clean-architecture.md"};function f(v,e,g,w,y,k){const c=a("LabArticleHeader"),d=a("Mermaid"),l=a("LabScroll");return i(),u("div",null,[e[1]||(e[1]=r("h1",{id:"robert-c-martin-principles-review",tabindex:"-1"},[o("Robert C. Martin principles review "),r("a",{class:"header-anchor",href:"#robert-c-martin-principles-review","aria-label":'Permalink to "Robert C. Martin principles review"'},"​")],-1)),t(c),e[2]||(e[2]=s('<p>A cancellation arrives just as an answer finishes. If the web component, HTTP handler, and worker each decide the outcome, a user may see one result while the stored conversation records another. This review asks where that decision should live.</p><p>It applies Robert C. Martin’s published architecture principles independently to a draft design. Martin did not participate in or endorse the work. The findings concern intended boundaries and failure behavior. No application code was reviewed.</p><h2 id="review-basis" tabindex="-1">Review basis <a class="header-anchor" href="#review-basis" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Review basis&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Martin&#39;s Clean Architecture separates policy from external mechanisms. Source dependencies point toward the policy layer; frameworks, transport, persistence, and presentation stay outside it. Boundary data should suit the inner application rather than expose a framework&#39;s objects. <a href="https://blog.cleancoder.com/uncle-bob/2012/08/13/the-clean-architecture.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Robert C. Martin, The Clean Architecture</a></p><p>In this system the long-lived rules cover evidence provenance, authorized retrieval, run transitions, cancellation intent, publication of document revisions, and decisions about entity identity. Put those rules where a test can exercise them without launching Axum, SvelteKit, SQLite extensions, NATS, a model provider, or a DAG engine.</p><h2 id="proposed-dependency-structure" tabindex="-1">Proposed dependency structure <a class="header-anchor" href="#proposed-dependency-structure" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Proposed dependency structure&quot;">​</a></h2>',6)),t(l,{kind:"diagram",label:"diagram for Proposed dependency structure"},{default:n(()=>[(i(),h(m,null,{default:n(()=>[t(d,{id:"mermaid-23",class:"mermaid",graph:"flowchart%20BT%0A%20%20accTitle%3A%20Dependency%20direction%20in%20the%20proposed%20system%0A%20%20accDescr%3A%20Frameworks%20call%20adapters.%20Adapters%20call%20application%20use%20cases%20or%20implement%20application-owned%20ports.%20Application%20code%20depends%20on%20the%20domain%20and%20its%20own%20ports.%0A%20%20%20%20Frameworks%5BAxum%20%2F%20SQLite%20%2F%20NATS%20%2F%20model%20SDKs%5D%20--%3E%20Adapters%5BTransport%20and%20persistence%20adapters%5D%0A%20%20%20%20Adapters%20--%3E%20Application%5BStartRun%20%2F%20RetrieveEvidence%20%2F%20PublishRevision%5D%0A%20%20%20%20Application%20--%3E%20Domain%5BRun%20%2F%20DocumentRevision%20%2F%20Claim%20%2F%20Evidence%5D%0A%20%20%20%20Adapters%20--%3E%20Ports%5BApplication-owned%20capability%20ports%5D%0A%20%20%20%20Application%20--%3E%20Ports%0A"})]),fallback:n(()=>[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o(" Loading... ",-1)])]),_:1}))]),_:1}),e[3]||(e[3]=s('<p>Arrows show source dependencies. Runtime calls may travel outward through an application-owned port. Rust traits and ordinary data structures can express this boundary; an additional service or allocation is not required merely to represent a layer.</p><h2 id="findings-and-required-design-changes" tabindex="-1">Findings and required design changes <a class="header-anchor" href="#findings-and-required-design-changes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Findings and required design changes&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="p1-—-define-the-run-protocol-before-splitting-services" tabindex="-1">P1 — Define the run protocol before splitting services <a class="header-anchor" href="#p1-—-define-the-run-protocol-before-splitting-services" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;P1 — Define the run protocol before splitting services&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The current service inventory does not yet establish who may create, cancel, complete, or replace a generation attempt. If those rules are duplicated in Svelte components, Axum handlers, and workers, reconnect and cancellation can produce contradictory states.</p><p><strong>Remediation:</strong> define a run state machine and application commands first. The application layer owns legal transitions, attempt identity, ownership, and terminal outcomes. HTTP and broker adapters translate requests into those commands. Require duplicate-command, late-event, cancellation, and reconnect scenarios to produce the same valid state regardless of transport.</p><h3 id="p1-—-make-evidence-policy-an-application-contract" tabindex="-1">P1 — Make evidence policy an application contract <a class="header-anchor" href="#p1-—-make-evidence-policy-an-application-contract" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;P1 — Make evidence policy an application contract&quot;">​</a></h3><p>An extracted relationship needs its supporting document revision and source locations. If those constraints exist only in SQL or prompts, a replacement vector extension or extractor can return an answer that the rest of the system cannot verify.</p><p><strong>Remediation:</strong> define <code>EvidenceRef</code>, <code>DocumentRevision</code>, <code>ResolvedMention</code>, and <code>RelationClaim</code> contracts independent of database rows. Specify authorization scope, active-revision requirements, uncertainty, and evidence retraction. Retrieval adapters return eligible evidence; source filters and graph expansion must preserve that scope.</p><h3 id="p1-—-give-workflow-progression-one-owner" tabindex="-1">P1 — Give workflow progression one owner <a class="header-anchor" href="#p1-—-give-workflow-progression-one-owner" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;P1 — Give workflow progression one owner&quot;">​</a></h3><p>A job completes while a retry is already queued. If both NATS and the DAG engine decide which work should run next, the project can pay twice for extraction or publish an artifact from an old input. Ask which component can inspect current state and make the one authoritative decision.</p><p><strong>Remediation:</strong> choose one durable owner for workflow state and dependency eligibility. Workers commit idempotent outputs identified by source revision, stage, input dependencies, and processing version. Publication checks the current revision. Events notify consumers about committed results; broker delivery does not define artifact validity.</p><h3 id="p2-—-keep-database-optimization-behind-useful-capability-boundaries" tabindex="-1">P2 — Keep database optimization behind useful capability boundaries <a class="header-anchor" href="#p2-—-keep-database-optimization-behind-useful-capability-boundaries" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;P2 — Keep database optimization behind useful capability boundaries&quot;">​</a></h3><p>A universal database repository abstraction could hide precisely the vector filtering, BM25 ranking, and graph-expansion behavior that determines retrieval quality. Conversely, leaking virtual-table SQL into run orchestration would couple policy to one extension.</p><p><strong>Remediation:</strong> define focused capabilities such as lexical candidate retrieval, eligible vector candidates, bounded relationship expansion, and revision publication. Specify observable semantics and quality requirements. Keep SQL and extension tuning in adapters. Avoid one interface for every table or a promise that different indexes have identical ranking behavior.</p><h3 id="p2-—-bound-modules-before-adding-network-boundaries" tabindex="-1">P2 — Bound modules before adding network boundaries <a class="header-anchor" href="#p2-—-bound-modules-before-adding-network-boundaries" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;P2 — Bound modules before adding network boundaries&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Separating policy from mechanisms does not establish a need for a microservice per layer. Every remote hop adds serialization, queueing, failure, and cancellation behavior to evaluate.</p><p><strong>Remediation:</strong> keep the live answer path in a cohesive Rust service with internal modules until isolation or measured scale warrants a split. Separate resource-heavy document processing because it needs independent capacity. Justify later boundaries through failure isolation, ownership, or workload evidence.</p><h2 id="strengths-to-retain" tabindex="-1">Strengths to retain <a class="header-anchor" href="#strengths-to-retain" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Strengths to retain&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The draft already separates live chat from enrichment, distinguishes searchable text from graph readiness, and treats graph claims as derived artifacts. Direct browser streaming avoids unnecessary orchestration in the answer path. These choices make failure domains and application policies easier to inspect.</p><h2 id="acceptance-gate" tabindex="-1">Acceptance gate <a class="header-anchor" href="#acceptance-gate" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Acceptance gate&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Before the first backend implementation is considered architecturally sound, demonstrate application-level scenarios using in-memory adapters: duplicate start commands, cancellation racing completion, stale document publication, entity-resolution correction, and evidence deletion. Then run adapter contract checks for the selected database and broker. These checks target policy failures; they do not assert that a layering diagram guarantees performance or correctness.</p>',21))])}const P=p(b,[["render",f]]);export{x as __pageData,P as default};
